WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.139 --> 00:00:04.320
In this part of optimization view modes,

2
00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:06.280
I'd like to talk about lighting complexity

3
00:00:06.280 --> 00:00:09.660
as lighting complexity shows you how many non-static lights

4
00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:11.960
you have affecting your geometry.

5
00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:13.519
Remember, a non-static light is

6
00:00:13.519 --> 00:00:18.120
a light that is dynamic or can move around and interact

7
00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:20.320
with the scene in real time.

8
00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:22.079
Now, the more non-static lights

9
00:00:22.079 --> 00:00:24.640
you have affecting any piece of geometry,

10
00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:28.980
the more expensive it will be to render.

11
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:32.000
Red means areas are going to have performance issues.

12
00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:35.560
And as we start to hit red to light purple, dark purple,

13
00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:37.439
and then on to white, this is going

14
00:00:37.439 --> 00:00:39.520
to be very expensive to render.

15
00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:42.439
So this is definitely something we need to look at.

16
00:00:42.439 --> 00:00:45.600
Now, again, as with most of our optimization view modes,

17
00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:49.079
the goal here is to identify possible areas

18
00:00:49.079 --> 00:00:53.520
and see what we can do to help adjust them in order

19
00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:56.039
to get some performance back.

20
00:00:56.100 --> 00:00:59.939
However, we don't want to make black and white decisions.

21
00:00:59.939 --> 00:01:02.259
For example, we don't need to have

22
00:01:02.259 --> 00:01:04.739
the scene that's ideal to moderate,

23
00:01:04.739 --> 00:01:07.980
as the car is the only object in the scene

24
00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:08.980
at this time.

25
00:01:08.980 --> 00:01:11.500
So because of that, we can get away

26
00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:14.939
with going to the more expensive and very expensive.

27
00:01:14.939 --> 00:01:16.659
And also, every scene is unique.

28
00:01:16.659 --> 00:01:18.700
So again, these tools are used

29
00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:20.900
to help us make better judgments.

30
00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:22.920
And let's go ahead and jump into the engine

31
00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:24.900
